Purpose

This document describes the exploratory analysis performed on the Farfetch product listings dataset in order to understand its structure, content, and suitability for NLP-based attribute extraction.

Dataset Overview

Source: Farfetch listings dataset (via Kaggle)

Format: CSV

Domain: Luxury fashion e-commerce

Primary NLP field: shortDescription

The dataset contains structured metadata alongside short textual descriptions that summarize each product.

Key Columns

shortDescription: short noun-phrase description of the product (primary NLP signal)

brand.name: brand metadata

gender: target gender category

priceInfo.*: pricing metadata

availableSizes: structured sizing information

images.*: image URLs

Only shortDescription is used as model input; other columns are used for analysis and validation.

Text Characteristics

Descriptions are short (typically 2–6 tokens)

Mostly noun phrases, not full sentences

Strong use of compound phrases (e.g. “shoulder bag”, “logo print”)

High repetition of fashion-specific terminology

This structure is well-suited for Named Entity Recognition (NER) rather than classification.

Distribution Analysis
Brand Distribution

Highly skewed toward luxury brands (e.g. Gucci, Prada, Fendi)

Long-tail distribution of smaller brands

Brand frequency influenced schema decisions but brands are not inferred from metadata

Gender Distribution

Majority of products labeled as women

Smaller but meaningful portion labeled unisex

Gender appears occasionally in text, but mostly exists as metadata

N-gram Insights

Analysis of unigrams and bigrams from shortDescription revealed:

High-frequency product nouns: dress, bag, jacket, sneakers

Frequent bigrams: shoulder bag, logo print, midi dress

Clear phrase-level attributes rather than isolated tokens

These observations directly informed the decision to use phrase-level BIO tagging.

Data Quality Checks

No duplicated rows detected

No missing values in shortDescription

Text is generally clean and standardized

Key Takeaways

The dataset is highly suitable for NER

Attributes are explicitly mentioned rather than implied

Phrase-level extraction is more appropriate than document classification

These insights motivated the schema design in Phase 3.
